WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:01.040
Good Morning Hank, it's Tuesday

00:00:01.040 --> 00:00:03.920
I'm just back from a trip to northern Jordan, with UNHCR,

00:00:03.920 --> 00:00:05.200
the UN refugee agency,

00:00:05.200 --> 00:00:09.800
where I got to know a few of the 635,000 Syrian refugees currently living in Jordan,

00:00:09.800 --> 00:00:12.640
and in the coming weeks I'll be sharing several of their stories.

00:00:12.640 --> 00:00:17.080
But as today is International Women's Day I want to focus on these two girls:

00:00:17.080 --> 00:00:19.940
12-year-old Majeeda and her 10-year-old sister Aida.

00:00:19.940 --> 00:00:24.320
Aida and Majeeda arrived at the Azraq Refugee Camp the day before I met them.

00:00:24.340 --> 00:00:28.240
but before that they'd spent more than 4 months in limbo at the border.

00:00:28.240 --> 00:00:33.620
Their father, Abu Majed, described the situation there as "cold, hunger and constant thirst."

00:00:33.620 --> 00:00:35.540
That morning they registered in the camp

00:00:35.540 --> 00:00:39.600
which involves everything from iris scans  to identify them, to finding an available shelter -

00:00:39.600 --> 00:00:43.080
- try getting a 4-year-old boy to stand  still for an iris scan, by the way.

00:00:43.080 --> 00:00:46.760
When I asked Aida and Majeeda's parents what the family's most urgent needs were

00:00:46.760 --> 00:00:49.920
they answered only that they wanted the kids to get into school

00:00:49.920 --> 00:00:52.560
and this was a theme among almost every refugee I met.

00:00:52.560 --> 00:00:55.840
The day before in the Za'atri Refugee Camp I met with the Tiger Group

00:00:55.840 --> 00:00:57.480
in which girls are mentored by young women

00:00:57.480 --> 00:01:03.480
and when I asked the girls what they wanted most, Aryam and Farah both answered immediately "To Learn"

00:01:03.480 --> 00:01:06.280
When I asked Nahid, a mother of young children who volunteers as a teacher

00:01:06.280 --> 00:01:08.680
what she wants for her kids she said: I want them to have a good education.

00:01:10.760 --> 00:01:14.840
Syria used to be a well educated nation, but the civil war has changed that.

00:01:14.840 --> 00:01:18.200
Aida has never been to school, she was only 5 when the war began.

00:01:18.200 --> 00:01:20.680
Majeeda has also been out of school for many years .

00:01:20.680 --> 00:01:24.160
When I later asked Aida what she wanted now that she was safe in the camp

00:01:24.160 --> 00:01:27.480
she didn't say electricity, or food, or rest, she said: We need to study.

00:01:28.520 --> 00:01:31.480
And while they will be able to study at the camp, 
it won't be easy:

00:01:31.480 --> 00:01:35.440
the schools are underfunded which means that 
classes only meet for half of each day

00:01:35.440 --> 00:01:40.840
and class sizes are often huge: there are sometimes over 75 students in a primary school classroom.

00:01:40.840 --> 00:01:45.160
Nonetheless, school will be welcome. I mean, for the past four months these kids had to walk

00:01:45.160 --> 00:01:47.640
10km almost every day to find firewood at the border,

00:01:47.640 --> 00:01:51.240
and before that they moved around Syria for years trying to stay safe.

00:01:51.240 --> 00:01:56.920
When I asked Aida if she'd lost friends in the war, she smiled, and nodded, and started to try to explain

00:01:56.920 --> 00:01:58.000
but then she began to cry.

00:01:58.000 --> 00:02:00.880
I felt terrible even for having asked the question,

00:02:00.880 --> 00:02:02.600
but then again, I want you to know.

00:02:02.600 --> 00:02:05.760
I want you to know that there should be another sister here.

00:02:07.200 --> 00:02:10.540
And yet I also want you to know that Aida and Majeeda have rich complex lives

00:02:10.540 --> 00:02:12.880
that aren't mere tragedies:

00:02:12.880 --> 00:02:15.640
they're friendly and warm kids and  they smiled a lot

00:02:15.640 --> 00:02:18.680
especially later in the day 
when I visited them in their shelter,

00:02:18.680 --> 00:02:20.320
a small but safe single room.

00:02:20.320 --> 00:02:22.400
They joked with each other, and played with their little brothers,

00:02:22.400 --> 00:02:25.640
they taught me their favorite childhood game, a Syrian version of hide-and-seek...

00:02:25.640 --> 00:02:29.320
And when I asked Aida what message she would like to share with American children her age

00:02:29.320 --> 00:02:32.920
our interpreter Nida told me she said: 
"We just want to go to school"

00:02:33.040 --> 00:02:34.440
"We just wanna have a life."

00:02:34.480 --> 00:02:37.560
This is not such a big ask, of course, 
but it's hard to deliver.

00:02:37.560 --> 00:02:39.760
The UN Refugee Agency remains critically underfunded

00:02:39.760 --> 00:02:42.800
and Syrian refugees aren't legally allowed to work in Jordan

00:02:42.800 --> 00:02:45.880
where the unemployment rate was already high even before the crisis.

00:02:45.880 --> 00:02:51.000
So education isn't the only challenge; 
I mean, when I asked Nahid about the unique difficulties female refugees face, she said:

00:02:56.600 --> 00:03:01.560
Syrian refugees have seen the time needed for unpaid work, from cooking to cleaning, increase dramatically

00:03:01.560 --> 00:03:05.120
And that work, as elsewhere in the world, is disproportionately done by women.

00:03:05.120 --> 00:03:09.280
Kids are often pulled from school to help with chores or find whatever paid work they can.

00:03:09.280 --> 00:03:14.560
That shortens the number of years of formal education kids receive
and narrows their future career opportunities

00:03:14.560 --> 00:03:19.040
and this is what people mean when they talk about a lost generation of Syrian youth.

00:03:19.040 --> 00:03:23.600
But of course, they aren't lost, because we know where they are: they are here in Azraq,

00:03:23.600 --> 00:03:26.200
they are waiting in desperate conditions on the border,

00:03:26.680 --> 00:03:29.960
they are in Germany, in Sweden, in Lebanon, in Turkey,

00:03:29.960 --> 00:03:31.680
and most of all, in Syria.

00:03:31.680 --> 00:03:35.720
And we must help them get the nutrition, shelter and education they need

00:03:35.720 --> 00:03:38.680
to grow into a generation that will help rebuild their country.

00:03:38.680 --> 00:03:43.840
Investing in girls like Aida and Majeeda isn't just good for them, it's vital for all of us.

00:03:43.840 --> 00:03:48.840
We can do this by supporting UNHCR in their critical and underfunded mission to support refugees around the world,

00:03:48.840 --> 00:03:52.600
but we also do it by acknowledging the full humanity of refugees.

00:03:52.600 --> 00:03:55.480
As Aida told me, We just want to have a life.

00:03:55.480 --> 00:04:00.400
But you can't have a life when the World doesn't accept that you are a person as valuable as any other,

00:04:00.400 --> 00:04:02.920
when the World treats you as "other", or "less than".

00:04:02.920 --> 00:04:05.280
We must fight that dehumanization

00:04:05.280 --> 00:04:10.640
not least because to see the humanity in others is to glimpse it within ourselves.

00:04:10.640 --> 00:04:12.160
Hank, I'll see you on Friday.

